lines=['# MG179 correlation correction and displaced-Y audit','', 'This stage resolved two different measurement problems at the design level. A new background diagnostic fits time correlation and noise variance together, removing the excessive clean-background rejections seen in MG178 and passing a predefined check on independent Gaussian references. The displaced-Y audit found useful alignment information in the saved field, but the current tracker does not explicitly represent a core whose X and Y features have separated. That incomplete hypothesis set can produce a confident-looking single-core label. The next instrument should combine the corrected background test with an explicit component-alignment alternative. No earlier labels or persistence verdicts changed, and a revised complete tracker is not yet qualified.','', '## Scope and prospective record','', 'Source, seed, inputs, budgets and criteria were published at `93d2d07` before independent validation. Deterministic expected-statistic checks passed at correlations 0, 0.8 and 0.95. Work used one worker and numerical-library thread, within the 600-second main and 120-second check limits. No Model G trajectories were generated. The new Gaussian samples are a statistical calibration of background references, not particle simulations.','', '## Correlation-uncertainty correction','', 'MG178 estimated correlation first and then treated that estimate as fixed when comparing temporal noise variance with an independent spatial reference. A small correlation error changes the conversion from innovation variance to stationary variance, especially near correlation 0.95. Its nominal F test did not account adequately for that effect.','', 'The new `mg179_profile.py` diagnostic uses the full stationary Gaussian AR1 likelihood, including the first observation and covariance determinant. It compares a shared spatial/temporal stationary variance with separate variances, fitting correlation separately under each alternative. This allows correlation to adjust within both hypotheses instead of substituting one fixed estimate. The likelihood-ratio reference is asymptotic chi-square with one degree of freedom, not an exact finite-sample distribution.','', 'The validation used 3000 independent references at each true correlation 0, 0.8 and 0.95: 9000 total. Each has the same 998 whitened dimensions, 16 independent spatial samples and 64 time-ordered samples as the intended reference design. For each correlation, tail counts at 5%, 1% and 0.1% were checked by a predefined two-sided binomial test with threshold 0.01/9. The scientific threshold was not fitted to these outcomes.','', '| Correlation | Tail probability | Observed /3000 | Expected count | Binomial p |','|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
